frustrated relatives wait outside a  hospital in Ginny's capital Conakry  desperate for news and when the bodies  of their loved ones will be released we  came today to collect the bodies but the  Ministry of Health says they're not  ready to be released the families have  been mourning for 14 days we want them  to give us the bodies for burial the 11  protesters were killed earlier this  month during one of a number of  demonstrations against president Alfa  conde thousands of people have been  marching through the city week after  week angry about a possible  constitutional change that they say will  push Guinea into a dictatorship they  accused president Conde of wanting to  hold on to power for a third term for  them ten years of Condes government is  enough 81 year old Conde first came to  power in 2010 it was Ginny's first  democratic election after more than 50  years of mostly military rule since its  independence from France in 1958 former  leaders are worried about the future of  Guinea under conde I don't have the  words to express myself this deception  but what are we seeing on the ground it  could kill politics in Guinea a funeral  is sacred in our tradition our younger  brothers were killed how can the family  come here 14 days later with no chance  to have the funeral earlier the Ministry  of Health says the delay in releasing  the bodies is because it's pathologists  haven't finished performing autopsies  which it says will help uncover the real  causes and circumstances of their loved  ones deaths these relatives have no  faith in that process and they insist  the government is making an already  difficult situation even worse Victoria  Gaytan B al-jazeera
 

